  [38;5;153msuccess[39m, …); dropped keys are counted into [38;5;153m_dropped: n[39m so absence is [3mvisible[0m rather than silent. Values must be
  primitives — objects and arrays are dropped rather than walked, which kills the "logged the whole [38;5;153mformData[39m" class of
  leak outright. Strings truncate at 512 chars. On top of that, a tripwire: a key matching
  [38;5;153m/pass|secret|token|auth|cookie|card|ssn/i[39m is dropped and counted separately, so a near-miss is detectable even though
  the allowlist already covered it. Defense in depth, because this is the failure mode with teeth.

  One accepted risk to state plainly: [38;5;153merr.stack[39m passes through, since it's the entire point of error reporting, and some
  engines embed values in stack frames. Allowlisting can't reach inside a stack string. Flagging it rather than
  pretending it's solved.

  [1mThe logger must never become the outage.[0m [38;5;153mtransport.send[39m is wrapped in [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m inside core — a throwing or offline
  transport never propagates to the call site. Send failures are counted, never logged, because logging about logging
  recurses. A reentrancy flag enforces that. The browser transport is fire-and-forget: no [38;5;153mawait[39m anywhere in the submit
  path, so reporting can't add latency to a login. And the global handlers preserve crash semantics rather than papering
  over them — the browser [38;5;153merror[39m listener doesn't [38;5;153mpreventDefault()[39m, and Node's [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m logs, attempts a
  [3mbounded[0m flush, then [38;5;153mprocess.exit(1)[39m. A crash handler that swallows the crash trades a visible failure for a zombie
  process.

  [1mTests[0m ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m, unit only): core (level filtering, event shape, [38;5;153mchild()[39m merge, throwing transport contained);
  redact (each rule above, with the password-and-tripwire cases as the centerpiece); transport (fan-out, one member
  throwing doesn't stop others); node adapter (handlers registered against a fake emitter, injected flush/exit).

  [1mGap I want on the record:[0m you declined e2e, so the browser wiring has no automated coverage — and that wiring is the
  exact thing that fixes "errors vanish silently." Core and redaction will be well tested; whether a real thrown error
  in a real page actually reaches the service will rest on one manual check. That's a reasonable trade at this size, but
  it should be a decision, not a surprise.

  Now writing the spec.

